LibreTranslate for Collabora Online

LibreTranslate is a machine translation service that runs on your own
hardware, with models from the Argos Translate project. Collabora Online
talks to it over HTTP for the Translate command of Writer, the way it talks
to DeepL, and replaces the document text, or the selection, with the
translation. This directory builds the service and shows how to run it next
to Collabora Online.

Nothing of LibreTranslate is in this repository. The Dockerfile installs
the release from PyPI while the image builds, checking every package
against the hashes in requirements.txt, and runs the server as the user
"libretranslate". LibreTranslate is released under the AGPL 3; the licence
text is in the installed package. The translation models are fetched from
the Argos Translate package index while the image builds; the index
publishes neither hashes nor a licence for them, so check the terms before
you redistribute an image with the models in it.

Build and try it:

    docker build -t libretranslate docker/libretranslate
    docker run --rm -p 5000:5000 libretranslate
    curl -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
         -d '{"q":"The house is green.","source":"en","target":"de","format":"text"}' \
         http://127.0.0.1:5000/translate

The answer is the German sentence. The languages the server has models
for are listed at http://127.0.0.1:5000/languages.

Running it next to Collabora Online:

    cd docker/libretranslate
    docker compose up

compose.yaml starts the collabora/code image with the provider enabled and
pointed at the service, on a network the service shares with nothing else.
In coolwsd.xml the same two settings are

    <libretranslate>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <base_url>http://libretranslate:5000</base_url>
    </libretranslate>

The base URL is the root of the server; the provider appends /translate and
/languages. One provider translates at a time: with DeepL enabled as well,
translation.provider in coolwsd.xml says which, and empty takes the first
enabled one, DeepL before LibreTranslate. allowed_languages in coolwsd.xml
does not come into it: it limits the local writing aids loaded at start,
and the Translate dialog lists what the server has.

Every model translates to or from English, so a translation between two
other languages goes through English. The languages are chosen when the
image builds, with the LIBRETRANSLATE_LANGUAGES build argument (en,de,fr,
es,it,nl,pt by default): each direction is a package of some 100 MB, and
each loaded model takes a few hundred MB of memory. A paragraph takes a
CPU core for a few seconds; LT_THREADS in compose.yaml says how many are
translated at once. Collabora Online waits 30 seconds for each one.

The server has no authentication by default and no TLS. Anyone who can
reach it can send it text, so keep it on an internal network, as
compose.yaml does, and never on a public address. A server that must be
reachable more widely can demand a key: start it with LT_API_KEYS=true and
LT_REQUIRE_API_KEY_SECRET=true, keep its key database on a volume mounted
at /srv/libretranslate/db, create a key with

    docker compose exec libretranslate /srv/libretranslate/bin/ltmanage keys add 120

(the number is the requests per minute the key allows) and put the key in
api_key of the libretranslate block of coolwsd.xml.

The server settings show in the Options dialog only where the
administrator allows it, with allow_user_settings in coolwsd.xml, and only
while the provider is enabled there. With it, a user may point Collabora
Online at a server and key of their own.

Collabora Office, the desktop application, has the same provider and is
configured in its own Options dialog.
